(7050552, 1071609, 99263, '<td valign="top">An image that is well executed with excellent lighting and most definitely scored well below it''s actual worth whatever that "worth" is worth here on DPC.<br/>I actually opened a thread about this particular challenge theme whilst the challenge was still open for entries.<br/> <br/>You cannot "shoot from behind your subject"<br/>But you can "shoot your subject from behind"<br/> <br/>Your "subject" will always be in front of your lens irrespective of where you are.<br/>You can however shoot the "behind" of your "subject" but only if your "subject" has a "behind"<br/>Some "subjects" don''t have "behinds" such as for instance trees although it''s perfectly possible to hide "behind" a tree. <br/>If you''re interested to see the thread and the responses it evoked here it is:<br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/forum.php?action=read&amp;FORUM_THREAD_ID=1207714" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">//www.dpchallenge.com/forum.php?action=read&amp;FORUM_THREAD_ID=1207714</a></td>', 'An image that is well executed with excellent lighting and most definitely scored well below it''s actual worth whatever that "worth" is worth here on DPC.I actually opened a thread about this particular challenge theme whilst the challenge was still open for entries. You cannot "shoot from behind your subject"But you can "shoot your subject from behind" Your "subject" will always be in front of your lens irrespective of where you are.You can however shoot the "behind" of your "subject" but only if your "subject" has a "behind"Some "subjects" don''t have "behinds" such as for instance trees although it''s perfectly possible to hide "behind" a tree. If you''re interested to see the thread and the responses it evoked here it is://www.dpchallenge.com/forum.php?action=read&FORUM_THREAD_ID=1207714', '2013-06-10 11:57:45.000000', NULL, 0),
(7050556, 1071608, 99263, '<td valign="top">Baie mooi, uitsonderlike boom met aparte vormgewing en pragtig in beeld gebring. <br/>Ironies genoeg het jy ongeveer dieselfde idee oor hierdie challenge thema as ek.<br/>Sien my pos hieroor wat ek gemaak het terwyl die challenge nog oop was vir inskrywings.<br/> <br/>You cannot "shoot from behind your subject"<br/>But you can "shoot your subject from behind"<br/> <br/>Your "subject" will always be in front of your lens irrespective of where you are.<br/>You can however shoot the "behind" of your "subject" but only if your "subject" has a "behind"<br/>Some "subjects" don''t have "behinds" such as for instance trees although it''s perfectly possible to hide "behind" a tree.<br/>If you''re interested to see the thread and the responses it evoked here it is:<br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/forum.php?action=read&amp;FORUM_THREAD_ID=1207714" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">//www.dpchallenge.com/forum.php?action=read&amp;FORUM_THREAD_ID=1207714</a><br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2013-06-10 17:12:50.</i></td>', 'Baie mooi, uitsonderlike boom met aparte vormgewing en pragtig in beeld gebring. Ironies genoeg het jy ongeveer dieselfde idee oor hierdie challenge thema as ek.Sien my pos hieroor wat ek gemaak het terwyl die challenge nog oop was vir inskrywings. You cannot "shoot from behind your subject"But you can "shoot your subject from behind" Your "subject" will always be in front of your lens irrespective of where you are.You can however shoot the "behind" of your "subject" but only if your "subject" has a "behind"Some "subjects" don''t have "behinds" such as for instance trees although it''s perfectly possible to hide "behind" a tree.If you''re interested to see the thread and the responses it evoked here it is://www.dpchallenge.com/forum.php?action=read&FORUM_THREAD_ID=1207714', '2013-06-10 12:03:07.000000', '2013-06-10 18:12:50.000000', 0),
